e driver; Engine 2 (Cyclical) would mis-characterize steady recurring revenue as cyclical; Engine 5 (Binary) is irrelevant.\n- Key Data Tier Composition: Tier 1 (guidance, financials) ~75%; Tier 2 (market, options, short interest) ~10%; Tier 3 (HCM industry, competitive) ~10%; Tier 4 (ARPU, retention projections) ~5%. Model Confidence: HIGH on trailing data; MODERATE on FY2028+ forecasts, which are [Assumption]-driven.\n\n\u25a0 SECTION 1. MACRO CONTEXT\n\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\n- 10-Year UST: 4.71% \u2014 4.74%, leveling higher (Aug 21, 2026) [Actual \u2014 FRED DGS10 / TradingEconomics]\n- Fed Funds: 3.50%-3.75%, ON HOLD for 5 consecutive meetings (July 2026); market prices ~1-in-3 chance of a HIKE [Actual \u2014 TradingEconomics/FOMC]\n- Dollar (DXY): 98.6 \u2014 99.0, weak-to-softening [Actual \u2014 TradingEconomics, Aug 20, 2026]\n- Inflation (CPI): [Unconfirmed \u2014 search budget exhausted; implied by nominal 10Y at 4.71% and real-rate expectations, market-implied inflation ~2.2% [Estimate]]\n- Credit spreads: [Unconfirmed \u2014 not retrieved; proxy: HY OAS estimated ~350-400bps given policy stance [Assumption]]\n- Economic cycle position: LATE EXPANSION / EARLY-NORMALIZING \u2014 Fed on hold, flat workforce assumption inside PCTY's own FY2027 guidance [Actual \u2014 management assumption]\n- Global liquidity: NEUTRAL-TO-TIGHT \u2014 central banks on hold, QT still in effect [Estimate]\n\nStep 1-2. REGIME CLASSIFICATION (Probabilities must sum to 100%):\n- Goldilocks (low-ish rates + growth): 30% \u2014 soft-landing scenario persists, but inverted curve risk remains\n- Reflation (higher rates + growth): 35% \u2014 current regime; growth sticky enough to keep Fed on hold\n- Stagflation (high rates + low growth): 20% \u2014 sticky services inflation, flat payrolls\n- Recession (rates cut + unemployment up): 15% \u2014 lagged effect of 4.7% 10Y on SMB credit\n\nStep 1-3. MACRO SENSITIVITY (each axis -3 to +3):\n- Interest rate sensitivity: -2 (client-fund float income falls when rates drop; SMB credit costs rise when high)\n- Dollar sensitivity: +1 (minimal FX exposure; weak dollar is modest tailwind on client confidence)\n- Liquidity sensitivity: -1 (rate-sensitive valuation multiple; funding environment matters for SMB clients)\n- Economic cycle sensitivity: -2 (payroll volumes, new business formation, and hiring all slow in recession)\n- Commodity sensitivity: 0 (no direct input exposure)\n\nStep 1-4. MACRO ALIGNMENT:\nExpected Macro Impact = 0.30\u00d7(Goldilocks +1) + 0.35\u00d7(Reflation 0) + 0.20\u00d7(Stagflation -1) + 0.15\u00d7(Recession -2)\n= 0.30 \u2212 0.20 \u2212 0.30 = -0.20 \u2192 NEUTRAL-TO-MILD HEADWIND.\n- Net effect: Conviction -0.5 steps. Headwind is modest, but the FY2027 company guidance itself (flat workforce, two 25bp rate cuts in H2) embeds this macro caution.\n\n\u25a0 SECTION 1.5. BASE RATE & HISTORICAL PATTERN\n\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\nStep 1.5-1. BASE RATES (industry evidence):\n- B2B payroll/HCM software incumbent, 12+ years operating, 92%+ revenue retention: 5-YEAR SURVIVAL RATE \u2248 90-95% [Estimate \u2014 industry base rate]\n- Probability of maintaining 7%+ organic growth for 5 years: for a mid-market HCM leader, \u2248 50-60% [Estimate]\n- Probability of a software company with FCF margin >20% remaining FCF-positive for 5 years: \u2248 85% [Estimate]\n- Probability of M&A ($Aidora/Grayscale scale) achieving ROIC > WACC: \u2248 25-35% [Actual base rate \u2014 large/medium software M&A]\n- Probability of a 30x trailing P/E company delivering 10%+ annual returns over 3 years: base rate \u2248 35-45% [Estimate \u2014 general market evidence]\n\nStep 1.5-2. DEVIATION FROM BASE RATE:\nMy conclusion (IV $127 vs price $153, -19% MoS) deviates from the consensus-forward \"buy\" view. Justifications:\n(1) The FY2027 guidance step-down (11% \u2192 7% growth) shows a structural growth break, not a one-off [Actual];\n(2) SBC at 7.8% of revenue is a permanent economic cost that most street measures ignore [Actual];\n(3) 24.2% reported FCF margin includes a one-time tax benefit and excludes SBC; normalizing reduces owner FCF to ~24% of reported [Estimate].\nDeviation justified: 3 company-specific reasons provided. Base rate not rejected (survival high), but VALUATION base rate (mean reversion of excessive multiples) supports underperformance risk.\n\nStep 1.5-3. BASE RATE SHOCK CHECK:\n- My probability of \"PCTY delivering >10% annual total returns from $153\" is ~30% \u2014 versus base-rate 35-45% for such multiples. Ratio < 1.5x, no shock. No red flag triggered.\n\n\u25a0 SECTION 2. BUSINESS QUALITY (BUFFETT + LYNCH)\n\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\nStep 2-1. BUSINESS UNDERSTANDING:\nPaylocity provides cloud payroll, human capital management (HCM), finance, and IT workflow software to US mid-market employers (44,400 clients). Revenue is recurring subscription plus interest earned on client funds held in trust (float). It competes with ADP, Paychex, UKG, and increasingly Microsoft, but owns a defensible mid-market niche.\nUnderstandability: HIGH.\n\nStep 2-2. MOAT ASSESSMENT (0-3 each):\n- Intangible Assets: 2 (brand credible in mid-market; not a consumer franchise)\n- Switching Costs: 3 (payroll is mission-critical; switching disrupts tax filings, benefits, compliance)\n- Network Effects: 1 (broker channel creates some two-sided behavior, but limited)\n- Cost Advantage: 1 (not the lowest-cost provider)\n- Economies of Scale: 2 (mid-market niche scale; dev amortized across client base)\n- Regulatory Barriers: 1 (compliance complexity helps incumbents, but not unique)\n- Data Moat: 2 (payroll/tax data accumulates and improves compliance AI)\n- TOTAL: 12/21 \u2192 WIDE MOAT (low end).\nMoat Direction: NARROWING. Evidence: (1) Decelerating client growth (7% FY2026 vs historic double-digit); (2) Microsoft/AI bundling could commoditize workflow automation; (3) management's pivot to AI suggests defensive necessity [Estimate from web brief, no tier-1 disclosure of competitive win/loss].\n\nStep 2-3. LYNCH CLASSIFICATION: STALWART (transitioned from Fast Grower). Checkpoints: EPS growth ahead of revenue growth (yes \u2014 22.4% diluted EPS growth FY2026 vs 11% revenue); dividend not needed; buyback accretive (yes \u2014 3.1% share reduction); FCF conversion positive.\n\nStep 2-4. FINANCIAL STRENGTH SCORECARD:\n- Revenue CAGR 5yr: +22.8% [Arithmetic on filed series]\n- Last-4Q revenue growth: +11.0% [Actual \u2014 Q4 FY2026]\n- Operating margin: 21.8% latest, median 10.8% over 12 years [Arithmetic]\n- ROE: 21.97% [Actual vendor]\n- FCF margin: 24.2% (company definition); ~18% after SBC [Estimate]\n- Net debt: NET CASH of $191M (cash $272M - LT debt $81M) [Arithmetic]\n- Piotroski F-score: 7/9 evaluated [Arithmetic]\n- Accruals ratio: -0.0568 (income is cash-backed) [Arithmetic]\nVERDICT: EXCELLENT \u2014 but note that SBC-adjusted FCF conversion (OCF\u2192FCF) is only ~62% after subtracting SBC [$254M/$533M] [Estimate].\n\nStep 2-5. CAPITAL ALLOCATION SCORECARD (0-3 each, total 15):\n1. Buyback Efficiency: 3 \u2014 repurchased $398.1M in FY2026 at avg ~$142 (below current $152.77), 2.8M shares, reducing diluted count -3.1%; cumulative $698M since May 2024 [Actual]\n2. M&A Track Record: 2 \u2014 Aidora/Grayscale are subscale; management admits FY2027 margin headwinds; base rate of software M&A value creation is modest [Estimate]\n3. Capex Efficiency: 3 \u2014 incremental FCF margin >20%; OCF margin 30.1% [Actual]\n4. Rationality of Mix: 3 \u2014 organic R&D (14.5% of revenue) + small tuck-ins + buyback, no dividend, debt repayment of $81.3M [Actual]\n5. Guidance Accuracy: 3 \u2014 consistent beat-and-raise; FY2026 raised twice [Actual via press release]\n- Insider ownership 19.7%: +1 pt [Actual vendor]\n- Insider net buy/sell 6mo: [Unconfirmed \u2014 no data] \u2192 not scored\n- CAPITAL ALLOCATION TOTAL: 15/15 (with +1 ownership bonus already incorporated) \u2014 EXCELLENT\n\n\u25a0 SECTION 2.5. INTANGIBLES-ADJUSTED FINANCIALS\n\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\nStep 2.5-1. R&D CAPITALIZATION:\n- R&D ~14.5% of revenue = $257M FY2026 [Actual \u2014 company non-GAAP]. Capitalizing 50% over 4 years adds ~$128M to invested capital; amortization adds back ~$32M/yr to operating income. Adjusted OI \u2248 $386M + $32M = $418M [Estimate].\nStep 2.5-2. SBC TREATMENT:\n- SBC FY2026 = $139M [Actual]. Adjusted FCF = Company FCF $427.8M \u2212 SBC $139M \u2212 one-time tax benefit (~$35M) = $254M normalized owner earnings [Estimate]. SBC as % of revenue = 7.8%, trending down from 12.4% (2015) \u2192 moderate, but real.\nStep 2.5-3. OPERATING LEASE CAPITALIZATION:\n- Operating leases $52M (FY2026) [Actual \u2014 Section 6B]. Adding to net debt \u2192 Adjusted net debt = -$139M (still net cash).\nStep 2.5-4. BRAND/DATA ASSET: Data moat from payroll/tax data not separately capitalized; qualitative only.\nStep 2.5-5. ADJUSTED ROIC:\n- GAAP ROIC \u2248 NOPAT ($270M + est. interest $10M after-tax) / (Equity $1.221B + Debt $81M - Cash $272M) \u2248 $280M / $1.030B \u2248 27% [Estimate]\n- Adjusted ROIC (with R&D capitalized, SBC expensed): \u2248 $418M\u00d70.75 / ($1.221B + $0.081B + $0.128B \u2212 $0.272B) \u2248 $313M/$1.158B \u2248 27% [Estimate]\n- No gap >5pp; ROIC robustly > WACC (9.5%) by a wide margin \u2014 a true compounder.\nStep 2.5-6. OWNER EARNINGS (BUFFETT):\n- FY2026: NI $270M + D&A $112M \u2212 SBC $139M \u2212 maintenance capex ~$105M \u2212 \u0394WC (normalized $0) = $138M if using strict maintenance; OR normalized owner FCF as above \u2248 $254M when using OCF \u2212 capex \u2212 SBC \u2212 one-time tax. I USE the $254M as the true repeatable owner-earnings base for cross-check; the DCF uses an EBIT-based framework that yields equivalent economics.\n\n\u25a0 SECTION 3. VALUATION (ENGINE 1 \u2014 DCF + MULTIPLES)\n\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\nSTEP 3-1. DISCOUNT RATE DERIVATION:\n- Risk-free rate (10Y UST): 4.71% [Actual \u2014 FRED, 2026-08-21]\n- Equity risk premium: 4.75% [Assumption]\n- Beta: 1.05 [Assumption \u2014 vendor's 0.433 is implausible given 52-wk range $92.99-$182.83 and short interest 7%; a mid-cap software name with SMB exposure carries ~1.0 beta at minimum; I override vendor]\n- Cost of equity = 4.71% + 1.05 \u00d7 4.75% = 9.70% [Estimate]\n- Cost of debt pre-tax: 6.0% [Assumption \u2014 SOFR + 200bps on $81M credit facility; actual rate not disclosed [Unconfirmed]]\n- After-tax cost of debt: 6.0% \u00d7 (1-0.25) = 4.5%\n- Weights: E = $8.458B, D = $0.081B, so D/(D+E) = 0.9%\n- WACC = 9.70% \u00d7 0.991 + 4.50% \u00d7 0.009 = 9.65% \u2192 ROUNDED TO 9.5% [Estimate]\n\nSTEP 3-2. REVENUE FORECAST (each driver sourced):\n| FY | Revenue ($B) | Growth | Source |\n|----|--------------|--------|--------|\n| 2026A | 1.771 | +11.0% | [Actual \u2014 filed] |\n| 2027E | 1.887 | +6.6% | [Actual \u2014 guidance midpoint $1.880-1.895B, 2026-08-04] |\n| 2028E | 2.019 | +7.0% | [Assumption \u2014 consensus forward revenue growth 7.69% (yfinance); decelerating from guided 7%, holding at 7% for one year] |\n| 2029E | 2.140 | +6.0% | [Assumption \u2014 fade toward GDP+; float income declines with rate cuts] |\n| 2030E | 2.247 | +5.0% | [Assumption \u2014 mid-market HCM growth ~4-5% + modest share gain] |\n| 2031E | 2.348 | +4.5% | [Assumption \u2014 continued fade] |\n| 2032E | 2.442 | +4.0% | [Assumption \u2014 long-run nominal GDP] |\n| Terminal | \u2014 | 4.0% | [Assumption \u2014 must be \u2264 nominal GDP; 4.0% = 2% real + 2% inflation] |\n\nSTEP 3-3. MARGIN & CASH FLOW FORECAST:\n- Operating margin path: FY2026A 21.8% \u2192 FY2027E 22.5% (guided adj. EBITDA $690-700M incl. interest; backing out $103M float interest, ~$592M ex-float, less D&A ~$113M, SBC ~$147M, other ~$18M \u2192 implied GAAP OI ~$417M \u2248 22.1%; I add 0.4pp for operating leverage [Estimate]) \u2192 FY2032E 24.9% (management path of ~120bps/yr ex-accounting-change [Assumption])\n- Tax rate: 25% [Assumption \u2014 FY2026 effective ~28.2% [Arithmetic]; guides toward ~24-25% as one-time benefits normalize]\n- D&A: 6.0% of revenue (FY2026: $112M/$1,771M = 6.3%) [Assumption \u2014 flat-ish]\n- Capex (total, incl. capitalized software): 5.9% of revenue (company's own non-GAAP FCF reconciliation implies total capex \u2248 $105M = 5.9% of revenue; pack PP&E only was $36M, but the company's FCF bridge of $427.8M = OCF $533M \u2212 $105M capex confirms total) [Estimate]\n- \u0394Working capital: $0 \u2014 normalized. The pack shows FY2026 \u0394WC = -$104M, but this is driven largely by client-fund obligations (float), which is pass-through and not a permanent cash drain. Normalized SaaS working capital is slightly positive. [Assumption \u2014 key driver; disclosed]\n- SBC: contained within GAAP op margin; not an add-back.\n\nPRO FORMA FCF (Unlevered Free Cash Flow = NOPAT + D&A \u2212 Capex \u2212 \u0394WC):\n| FY | Rev ($M) | OI ($M) | NOPAT | D&A ($M) | Capex ($M) | \u0394WC | FCF ($M) |\n|----|----------|---------|-------|----------|------------|-----|----------|\n| 2026A | 1,771 | 386 | 290 | 112 | 105 | 0 | 297 |\n| 2027E | 1,887 | 424 | 318 | 113 | 111 | 0 | 320 |\n| 2028E | 2,019 | 464 | 348 | 121 | 119 | 0 | 350 |\n| 2029E | 2,140 | 503 | 377 | 128 | 126 | 0 | 379 |\n| 2030E | 2,247 | 539 | 404 | 135 | 133 | 0 | 406 |\n| 2031E | 2,348 | 575 | 431 | 141 | 139 | 0 | 433 |\n| 2032E | 2,442 | 608 | 456 | 147 | 144 | 0 | 459 |\n\nSTEP 3-4. DCF VALUATION:\nDiscount factors (WACC 9.5%, mid-period convention; first cash flow ~10 months from T0):\n- DF FY2027 = 1/(1.095^0.83) = 0.9274\n- DF FY2028 = 1/(1.095^1.83) = 0.8469\n- DF FY2029 = 1/(1.095^2.83) = 0.7734\n- DF FY2030 = 1/(1.095^3.83) = 0.7062\n- DF FY2031 = 1/(1.095^4.83) = 0.6450\n- DF FY2032 = 1/(1.095^5.83) = 0.5889\n\nPV of FCF: 296.8 + 296.4 + 293.1 + 286.7 + 279.3 + 270.3 = 1,722.6\nTerminal value (g=4.0%) = 459 \u00d7 1.04 / (0.095 - 0.04) = 477.4 / 0.055 = 8,680.2\nPV of TV = 8,680.2 \u00d7 0.5889 = 5,111.7\nEnterprise value = 1,722.6 + 5,111.7 = 6,834.3\nPlus net cash (cash $272M \u2212 LT debt $81M; operating leases $52M excluded but noted) = +191.3\nEquity value = 7,025.6\nPer share on 55.0M diluted shares = $127.7 \u2248 $128/share [Estimate]\n\nSTEP 3-5. MULTIPLE CROSS-CHECK (NOT the primary method \u2014 no DCF-only conclusion):\n- Forward P/E: FY2027E EPS ~$5.47 (OI $424M - interest $10M = pre-tax $414M \u00d7 0.72 = $298M / 54.5M shares) \u2192 P/E = 27.9\u00d7 [Estimate]\n- EV/Adj EBITDA ex-float: EV $8,458 + $81 - $272 = $8,267M / ~$592M = 14.0\u00d7 [Estimate]\n- P/Owner-FCF (normalized, SBC-adjusted): $8,458M / $320M = 26.4\u00d7 [Estimate]\n- Justified P/FCF = (1+g)/(r-g) = 1.04 / 0.055 = 18.9\u00d7 \u2192 fair EV = 320\u00d718.9 = $6,048M \u2192 + $191M cash = $6,239M \u2192 $113/share [Estimate]\n- Peers: ADP ~24-26\u00d7 fwd P/E; Paychex ~20-22\u00d7 [Estimate \u2014 no direct retrieval; base knowledge]. PCTY at 27.9\u00d7 is a premium to peers despite faster deceleration. No support for premium.\n\nSTEP 3-6. TORNADO SENSITIVITY \u2014 TOP 3 VARIABLES:\n1. Terminal growth (g): \u00b11pp changes IV by \u00b112%/$15 (range $112-$142)\n2. WACC: \u00b11pp changes IV by \u00b116%/$21 (range $106-$148)\n3. FY2029+ operating margin: \u00b12pp changes IV by \u00b16%/$8\n\u2192 Thesis KPIs: revenue retention (drives terminal growth), hiring/payroll environment (drives WACC via macro), and AI module attach (drives margin).\n\n\u25a0 SECTION 3.5. SECOND-ORDER EFFECTS\n\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\n1. Competitive response (score -2): If PCTY's AI (Ignite AI) succeeds, ADP/Paychex will bundle equivalent AI at zero incremental cost, compressing ARPU. Microsoft's HCM integration in Teams is a structural risk [Estimate \u2014 web brief, unverified]\n2. Reflexivity (-1): High stock price has funded SBC-heavy talent acquisition; a falling price (down 16% from $182.83 high) could slow AI talent hiring, but 19.7% insider ownership buffers.\n3. Customer behavior evolution: Early-adopter AI usage is growing >100% QoQ [Actual \u2014 company comment], but mainstream SMB adoption is slower; LTV/CAC likely stable as broker channel (>25% of new business) improves CAC economics [Actual].\n\n\u25a0 SECTION 4. SCENARIOS (BAYESIAN)\n\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\nPriors (from 1.5): strong survival base rate; 50-60% chance of 7%+ 5-yr growth; valuation mean-reversion risk high.\n\nBEAR (25%) \u2014 \"AI disintermediation + SMB recession\":\n- Revenue growth: 6% FY2027, 4% FY2028, 3% thereafter; terminal g=3%\n- Operating margin: 22.0% plateau (no leverage; competition forces price concessions)\n- WACC: 10.5% (SMB credit stress raises risk premium)\n- FCF path: ~$304M declining to ~$291M by FY2032\n- IV \u2248 $65/share [Estimate]\n\nBASE (50%) \u2014 \"Stalwart compounder at fair value\":\n- Path as in Section 3 forecast\n- IV = $128/share [Estimate]\n\nBULL (25%) \u2014 \"AI attach re-accelerates growth\":\n- Revenue growth: 7% FY2027, 8% FY2028, 7% FY2029, 6% FY2030, 5.5% FY2031, 5% FY2032; terminal g=4%\n- Operating margin: reaches 2